WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.860
Digestion breaks large food molecules into small molecules that can be absorbed. Follow a meal from ingestion in the mouth to egestion through the anus.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:13.759
Accessory organs add secretions. Food does not pass through the liver, gallbladder or pancreas.

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:21.920
Ingestion brings food into the mouth. Teeth grind it, increasing surface area. The tongue mixes food with saliva and forms a bolus.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:29.502
Salivary glands add water, mucus and salivary amylase. Amylase starts starch digestion into maltose and shorter chains.

00:00:29.502 --> 00:00:35.977
Tongue glands release lingual lipase. This fat-digesting enzyme acts mainly after swallowing, in the stomach.

00:00:35.977 --> 00:00:40.889
The tongue pushes the bolus into the pharynx. The epiglottis helps protect the airway.

00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:47.991
Peristalsis, waves of muscle contraction, pushes food down the oesophagus. No digestive enzymes are added here.

00:00:47.991 --> 00:00:51.288
A sphincter admits food into the stomach and limits backflow.

00:00:51.288 --> 00:00:58.160
Stomach muscles churn food into chyme. Hydrochloric acid unfolds proteins and activates pepsin from pepsinogen.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:05.555
Pepsin cuts proteins into peptides. Acid is not an enzyme. Increasing acidity inactivates salivary amylase.

00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:14.481
Gastric and lingual lipases digest some fat. Mucus protects the lining. The stomach also produces intrinsic factor for later vitamin B12 absorption.

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:22.706
Chyme enters the duodenum. The pancreas supplies bicarbonate to neutralise acid, and enzymes to digest carbohydrates, proteins and fats.

00:01:22.706 --> 00:01:28.895
The liver makes bile. The gallbladder stores, concentrates and releases bile through ducts into the duodenum.

00:01:28.895 --> 00:01:36.358
Bile salts emulsify large fat droplets into smaller droplets, increasing surface area for lipase. Bile is not an enzyme.

00:01:36.358 --> 00:01:43.091
Pancreatic amylase continues starch digestion, producing maltose, maltotriose and branched fragments called dextrins.

00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:51.149
Intestinal surface enzymes must finish their breakdown into single sugars. Humans lack cellulase, so cellulose fibre can reach the colon.

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:58.825
The pancreas releases protein-digesting enzymes as inactive precursors. Intestinal enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen into trypsin.

00:01:58.825 --> 00:02:05.756
Trypsin activates other pancreatic proteases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase cut protein chains internally.

00:02:05.756 --> 00:02:12.247
Carboxypeptidases release amino acids from peptide ends. Intestinal peptidases complete digestion.

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:21.880
Pancreatic lipase breaks triglycerides mainly into fatty acids and monoglycerides. Colipase assists lipase; it is a helper protein, not a digestive enzyme.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:30.985
Phospholipase digests phospholipids. Cholesterol esterase acts on cholesterol esters. Bile salts carry fat products towards intestinal cells.

00:02:30.985 --> 00:02:36.512
Pancreatic RNase and DNase break dietary RNA and DNA into nucleotides.

00:02:36.512 --> 00:02:46.367
Brush-border enzymes finish digestion. Maltase and glucoamylase release glucose. Sucrase makes glucose and fructose; lactase makes glucose and galactose.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:54.374
Isomaltase, or alpha dextrinase, splits branched carbohydrate fragments. Trehalase digests trehalose into glucose.

00:02:54.374 --> 00:03:03.908
Aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish peptide digestion into amino acids. Phosphatases, nucleotidases and nucleosidases finish nucleotide digestion.

00:03:03.908 --> 00:03:06.760
These enzymes work on or near intestinal cells.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:14.167
The small-intestinal lining has folds and finger-like villi. Each villus contains blood capillaries and a central lymph vessel called a lacteal.

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:21.230
Tiny microvilli on absorptive cells form the brush border. Folds, villi and microvilli greatly increase surface area.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:27.252
The covering is one cell thick. Nutrients are absorbed throughout the small intestine, including the ileum.

00:03:27.252 --> 00:03:35.207
Single sugars and amino acids cross intestinal cells using transport proteins. They enter blood capillaries and travel to the liver in portal blood.

00:03:35.207 --> 00:03:41.253
Most long-chain fat products are rebuilt into triglycerides and packaged as chylomicrons inside intestinal cells.

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:48.974
Chylomicrons enter lacteals, travel through lymph, then reach blood. Shorter-chain fatty acids can enter portal blood directly.

00:03:48.974 --> 00:03:56.191
Water, minerals and vitamins are also absorbed. Cells use nutrients for energy, growth and repair: assimilation.

00:03:56.191 --> 00:04:00.595
The jejunum absorbs much of the digested food; the ileum continues absorption.

00:04:00.595 --> 00:04:06.938
The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor, and reabsorbs bile salts for return to the liver.

00:04:06.938 --> 00:04:10.958
Remaining material passes through the ileocaecal valve into the large intestine.

00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:18.944
The colon absorbs more water and salts, making contents firmer. It adds no human digestive enzymes, but bacteria supply their own.

00:04:18.944 --> 00:04:27.010
Bacteria ferment some fibre into short-chain fatty acids: acetate, propionate and butyrate. Butyrate fuels colon-lining cells.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:35.187
Microbes make vitamin K and some B vitamins, and help support the gut barrier and resist harmful microbes. Fermentation also produces gas.

00:04:35.187 --> 00:04:39.254
Remaining fibre, bacteria, shed cells and water form faeces.

00:04:39.254 --> 00:04:46.530
The rectum stores faeces. Stretching triggers a defecation reflex. Anal sphincters control release through the anus.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:54.279
This is egestion: removal of undigested and unabsorbed material. Excretion instead removes metabolic wastes, such as urea.

00:04:54.279 --> 00:05:00.000
Remember: ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation, then egestion.
